            <ArticleTitle>Radiographic and patient-based outcome analysis of different bone-grafting techniques in the surgical treatment of idiopathic scoliosis with a minimum 4-year follow-up: allograft versus autograft/allograft combination.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>523-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.spinee.2013.01.025</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1529-9430(13)00081-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Autograft and allograft have been equally successful in achieving arthrodesis, but whether there is any difference in their effect on patient outcome, especially early, has not been determined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine if autograft in addition to allograft is associated with decreased healing period pain, increased early function, or both.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">This is a retrospective comparative case series.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT SAMPLE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A sample of 47 patients, 20 years or younger with adolescent idiopathic scoliosis treated by the same surgeon at the same institution using third-generation segmental spinal instrumentation and arthrodesis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Function and pain were quantified at periodic intervals using the Scoliosis Research Society (SRS) health-related quality of life (HRQoL) questionnaire. Clinical and radiographic follow-ups were completed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Freeze-dried corticocancellous allograft (AL) was used in 26 patients and allograft plus iliac crest autograft (AL-AU) in 21 patients. Radiographs and outcome measures, including SRS-24 or SRS-22 HRQoL questionnaires, were obtained preoperatively and at intervals with a 4-year follow-up available for 92% (25/26) of AL patients and 90% (19/21) of AL-AU patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were no differences between the AL and AL-AU groups' ages, curve patterns, and complications. Neither group had a major complication or pseudoarthrosis. There were no main curve size differences at any interval. Pain scores were similar at all intervals: AL/AL-AU preoperative, 4.1/4.0; early follow-up (&lt;1 year), 3.7/4.1; midterm follow-up (1-2 years), 4.4/4.6; and late follow-up (&gt;3 years), 4.1/4.0. Function scores were also similar at all intervals: AL/AL-AU preoperative, 4.7/4.6; early follow-up, 4.2/4.3; midterm follow-up, 4.9/4.9; and late follow-up, 4.5/4.4.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The addition of autograft to allograft did not result in decreased pain or increased function at any time interval up to 4 years. We conclude that the addition of iliac crest autograft does not result in any advantage over freeze-dried allograft alone in the treatment of adolescent idiopathic scoliosis.</AbstractText>
